WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:04.920
Only one country has ever bombed the United&nbsp;
States with nuclear weapons: the United&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:10.320
States. For everything Oppenheimer gets right, it&nbsp;
ignores one tragic detail about the Trinity test.

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:14.400
In Christopher Nolan's Oppenheimer, the&nbsp;
Trinity Test at Los Alamos, New Mexico,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:19.980
is a triumph. It's a moment of pure success unique&nbsp;
in its separation from death and human suffering,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.980 --> 00:00:24.900
an isolated display of the genius simultaneously&nbsp;
praised and feared by the film's narrative.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:29.280
For one scene, and one scene only, the most&nbsp;
dangerous scientific discovery the world has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:34.440
ever known supposedly has no victims. However,&nbsp;
that could not be further from historical fact.

00:00:34.440 --> 00:00:37.320
"The earth trembled, you know,&nbsp;
when that thing exploded."

00:00:37.320 --> 00:00:40.980
There is likely no place in the world&nbsp;
where one could detonate a nuclear bomb&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.980 --> 00:00:45.000
and have it be harmless. It's true that&nbsp;
the blast zone at Los Alamos was clear,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:49.140
but neither the scientists in the film&nbsp;
nor their historical counterparts took&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.140 --> 00:00:52.920
into account how wind patterns would carry&nbsp;
the biblical amounts of radiation from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:56.880
their isolated scientific haven into&nbsp;
the unsuspecting communities nearby.

00:00:56.880 --> 00:00:59.880
The self-described Tularosa Basin Downwinders — or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.880 --> 00:01:04.260
Trinity Downwinders — are a community of&nbsp;
New Mexico citizens that, despite neither&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.260 --> 00:01:08.220
consenting nor even being informed of the&nbsp;
atomic bomb detonation near their homes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.220 --> 00:01:12.660
have been suffering the consequences&nbsp;
of the Trinity Test for over 75 years.

00:01:12.660 --> 00:01:17.820
As noted in Oppenheimer, Los Alamos was chosen&nbsp;
in part because of how remote of an area it was.

00:01:17.820 --> 00:01:22.560
"Why would we go to the middle of&nbsp;
nowhere for who knows how long?"

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:25.260
Scientists were confident that&nbsp;
those outside the blast radius&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.260 --> 00:01:27.900
would be safe from immediate&nbsp;
harm. The fallout, however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.900 --> 00:01:32.280
they were far less certain about. After&nbsp;
detonating the massive weapon, nuclear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.280 --> 00:01:37.500
fallout rained down from the blazing atmosphere&nbsp;
over a 200-square-mile area northeast of the test.

00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:40.860
This was particularly harmful&nbsp;
to the predominantly Hispanic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.860 --> 00:01:45.300
and Indigenous communities in rural New&nbsp;
Mexico, whose produce, drinking water,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.300 --> 00:01:51.180
and livestock were contaminated. Even after the&nbsp;
detonation, those affected weren't evacuated or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.180 --> 00:01:54.780
even notified by the U.S. government&nbsp;
of the atomic bomb, beginning a cycle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.780 --> 00:01:59.100
of death, struggle, and institutional&nbsp;
abandonment that continues to this day.

00:01:59.100 --> 00:02:06.420
"In the end, these people were betrayed by their&nbsp;
government. The ultimate sin in a democracy."

00:02:06.420 --> 00:02:12.000
Though the bomb ended America's war with Japan&nbsp;
in August 1945, the Trinity Downwinders' war&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:16.500
had just begun. In the years that followed,&nbsp;
exposure to traveling radiation and contaminated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:21.840
resources caused disturbing health problems&nbsp;
for New Mexicans in the affected area. Infant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.840 --> 00:02:27.840
mortality in the state skyrocketed by 56% over a&nbsp;
five-year period; rare diseases began to develop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.840 --> 00:02:32.520
in otherwise healthy, young individuals; and, of&nbsp;
course, cancer cases were abnormally prevalent.

00:02:32.520 --> 00:02:38.100
Surviving such illnesses was only half the battle.&nbsp;
Inhabitants of rural communities had to make long&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.100 --> 00:02:42.240
journeys to expensive doctors in order to&nbsp;
get the help they needed. If they were able&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.240 --> 00:02:47.400
to do so, they still faced difficulty finding and&nbsp;
maintaining employment as their symptoms worsened.

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:52.500
For many in the Tularosa Basin, medical hardships&nbsp;
were dwarfed by financial ones. It became common&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.500 --> 00:02:56.460
for parents to be unable to support their&nbsp;
children's college education. Given the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.460 --> 00:03:00.360
Trinity Test's role in their plight, it would&nbsp;
be reasonable to assume the government would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.360 --> 00:03:05.820
step in to provide assistance. Yet several decades&nbsp;
later, the victims are still fighting to be heard.

00:03:05.820 --> 00:03:12.420
"The only country that ever bombed our citizens&nbsp;
with nuclear weapons was our own government."

00:03:12.420 --> 00:03:18.600
In 1990, the Radiation Exposure Compensation Act&nbsp;
or RECA was passed to provide financial assistance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.600 --> 00:03:23.280
to those struggling from the effects of radiation&nbsp;
poisoning caused by governmental nuclear testing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.280 --> 00:03:29.160
in the 1950s. Unfortunately, this act failed to&nbsp;
include the victims of the 1945 Trinity Test,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.160 --> 00:03:34.080
and attempts to rectify this have been rejected&nbsp;
by the U.S. government for decades. The issue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.080 --> 00:03:39.000
is especially dire at this moment. Less than a&nbsp;
year after the release of Oppenheimer, the RECA&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:43.680
program will expire, and there will be no more&nbsp;
compensation for harm caused by nuclear testing.

00:03:43.680 --> 00:03:48.180
Thankfully, a group of activists known as&nbsp;
the Tularosa Basin Downwinders Consortium,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.180 --> 00:03:53.880
or TBDC, scored a victory in July 2023&nbsp;
when an official map of the Trinity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.880 --> 00:03:58.200
Test fallout was finished and released to&nbsp;
the public. In an interview with Axios,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.200 --> 00:04:02.460
TBDC co-founder Tina Cordova&nbsp;
described Oppenheimer, saying"

00:04:02.460 --> 00:04:06.480
"[The movie is] nothing but an over-glorification&nbsp;
of the science and the scientists, again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:09.480
with no reflection on the harm&nbsp;
done to the people in New Mexico."

00:04:09.480 --> 00:04:13.800
"The test actually produced more&nbsp;
light and more heat than the sun,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.800 --> 00:04:17.100
so you can imagine what that&nbsp;
experience was like for them."

00:04:17.100 --> 00:04:21.180
Despite many attempts by Cordova and other&nbsp;
survivors to contact the production in hope&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.180 --> 00:04:25.020
of convincing them to include some mention&nbsp;
acknowledging "the sacrifice and suffering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.020 --> 00:04:29.940
of the people of New Mexico," no one from&nbsp;
Oppenheimer reportedly responded or reached out.

